{"messages":[{"content":"You are the Accuracy critic. Flag any claim that is wrong, overstated\nor unsupported. Answer `APPROVE` if every claim is sound, otherwise\n`REVISE: ` and the claim to fix. Your first word must be APPROVE or REVISE.\n","role":"system"},{"content":"The bicycle's history began in 1817, when Karl von Drais introduced the \"draisine,\" a steerable, two-wheeled machine riders propelled with their feet. French inventors added cranks and pedals in the 1860s, producing the heavy \"boneshaker\" velocipede, followed by the iconic high-wheeled penny-farthing of the 1870s, whose huge front wheel offered speed but little safety. The modern bicycle emerged in 1885 with John Kemp Starley's Rover, the \"safety bicycle,\" which featured equal-sized wheels and a chain drive, and John Boyd Dunlop's pneumatic tires in 1888 greatly improved comfort. These innovations transformed the bicycle from an eccentric contraption into a reliable, affordable machine that revolutionized personal transportation and, by the 1890s, sparked a global cycling craze.","role":"user"}],"model":"test-model"}HTTP/1.1 200 OK
